    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> reformists who built an empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#senegal-river">Senegal River</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebro-river">Ebro</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>.</li><li>Their victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sagrajas">Sagrajas</a> in 1086 halted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>'s advance and kept much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> Muslim for generations.</li><li>Overstretched on two fronts, they fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, who used the same charge of impiety the Almoravids once used.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Almoravid gold dinar was so trusted that Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> imitated it as the morabetino, the origin of the Castilian maravedí.</p></aside>
    </section>

